\section{Published code on our splits}
\label{app:public}

\paragraph{What was run.}
We ran the published anchor code on our folds and targets (its $k$-means random states are $0$ to $9$) at $n=100$ with $10$ draws, as a check on our re-implementations; the code was run, not copied into ours.
The tinyBenchmarks code~\citep{maiapolo2024tiny} gives anchors by $k$-means on the sources' correctness columns (\emph{correctness anchors}) or on the item parameters of a multidimensional two-parameter IRT model fitted with its fork of \texttt{py-irt} (ten dimensions, hierarchical priors, $2000$ epochs; \emph{IRT anchors}), each with cluster-mass weights, and the p-IRT estimator, which fits the target's ability by maximum likelihood on the anchor answers and adds the model's prediction for the other items to the observed fraction.
Anchor Points~\citep{vivek2024anchor} gives $k$-medoids anchors on the item correlations, with cluster-size weights.
The anchor estimators without IRT cover the seven leaderboard benchmarks, all five folds in interpolation and the extrapolation fold; those with IRT cover six (not MMLU-Pro), the first fold in interpolation and the extrapolation fold.
We did not run the Anchor Points predictor or gp-IRT.
Each cell is one run, so there are no intervals; the ratio is the RMSE over that of uniform $\times$ mean on the same targets, as a geometric mean over the benchmarks.

\paragraph{Results.}
Table~\ref{tab:public} gives the ratios.
The published anchors chosen by correctness and Anchor Points are worse than uniform sampling, as our anchor design with the sample mean is: in extrapolation they give $2.49$ and $1.51$, against $1.91$ for ours, and in interpolation $1.14$ and $1.38$ against $1.07$; stratified sampling is $0.84$ and $0.79$ on the same benchmarks.
The published anchors chosen from the IRT embeddings, with their weights and no IRT model, are close to uniform in extrapolation ($0.94$) and below it in interpolation ($0.82$), against $1.99$ and $1.09$ for ours, on six benchmarks.
With IRT, in extrapolation p-IRT on the IRT anchors is $0.88$ and on the correctness anchors $1.05$, against $2.10$ for our anchor design with the IRT plug-in and $0.85$ for stratified $\times$ mean; per benchmark the IRT anchors give less than $1$ on five of the six (BBH is $1.77$), while our plug-in is above $1$ on all six.
In interpolation p-IRT is $0.69$ with either anchor set, against $0.79$ for our plug-in and $0.62$ for our anchor design with the difference estimator.
So the failure of an IRT predictor above the sources that the main text reports is a property of the IRT model we fitted, not of every IRT method, and no published method on this subset is clearly below stratified sampling there.

