\paragraph{The setting of our IRT model.}
Our model uses two dimensions, chosen on the sources only, and a standard normal prior on the target's ability, where the published code uses ten dimensions and no ability prior.
On the same six benchmarks and the extrapolation fold, our anchor design with the IRT plug-in is $2.10$ at two dimensions, $1.94$ at ten dimensions, $1.99$ at two dimensions with a prior variance a hundred times larger (the weak prior of Appendix~\ref{app:recal} is ten times larger) and $2.01$ at ten dimensions with it; on a uniform design the plug-in is $1.60$, $2.00$, $1.47$ and $2.04$.
A longer fit (2000 Adam steps instead of 300 and item and ability penalties a tenth as large) does not help: with the default prior the plug-in on the anchor design is $2.22$ at two dimensions and $3.38$ at ten, and on a uniform design $1.98$ and $3.04$; with the wide prior it is $2.11$ and $3.52$ on the anchor design and $1.92$ and $3.11$ on a uniform design.
Neither the dimension, the ability prior nor the length of the fit, alone or together, brings our estimator near the published one.
We did not test the hierarchical priors on the items, maximum-likelihood ability without a prior, the estimator form of the published code or its anchor set in our fitter; we state no cause.
